The U.S. refuted all its claims, refusing to extradite Gülen and advocivating to change the tone.
The last statement of the representatives of the state Department in this matter sounded at the time of Erdogan’s visit to St. Petersburg last Tuesday.
However, this does not stop the President of Turkey - he steadfastly insists on his and also puts forward demands on its other Western partners.
“Ankara ceases to fulfill its obligations within the framework of a migration agreement with the European Union, if Brusssel does not give a date for the abolition of visa restrictions for Turkish citizens”, – said on August 9, the Minister of Turkey for EU Affairs Omer Celick.
According to him, Ankara’s introduction of amendments to the anti-terrorist law, as demanded by the EU, will jeopardize Europe’s own security.
It should be noted that on August 1, German vice-chancellor Sigmar Gabriel demanded Turkey to stop the blackbang of the European Union with promises to open borders for migrants in order to achieve visa facilitation with the EU.
His words became a response to the corresponding statement of the Turkish Foreign Minister Mevlut Cavusoglu.
Cavuşoğlu made another provococative statement last Wednesday, admitting that Turkey could withdraw from NATO.
The head of the Turkish foreign Ministry did not preclose that the country will consider the possibility of withdrawing from the alliance, if it does not meet the expected support from it.
The decision to increase cooperation with Russia in the defense sector is not a step against NATO.
There are NATO countries that are cooperating with foreign states in the defense sector - they import missiles," Cavusoglu said in an interview with Anadolu.
According to him, Turkey now needs to create its own defense system on the basis of its own technologies, as well as to cooperate with other countries.
“Such so far, Turkey has tried to cooperate with NATO member countries, but the results didn’t satisfy us, and we began to look for others,” he said.
Experts believe that all of this speaks about Turkey's serious disagreements with the West and the reorientation of the country to the East.
The President of the International Institute of Political Expert Opportunity Yevgeny Minchenko believes that Recep Tayyip Erdogan is now playing his own game and can not go to a tough confrontation with the West.
He is very much tied to the West both in terms of business and from the point of view of military (Turkey is a member of NATO), so I think that Erdogan will try to balancing while maintaining framework relations with the West.
Although, of course, it would be more convenient for the Americans to see a completely obscene person at this post, ”the expert explained.
According to him, it is possible that after the statements of the Turkish leader (on the non-replenishment of the compensation of the EU, about the demand for visa-free space with Europe and the issuance of Gülen from the United States) will put pressure on him.
“Recep Tayychief will face a deteriorating relationship with both the United States and the European Union, and they will put pressure on him on humanitarian issues, for dictatorial methods of governing the country and so on,” Minchenko said.
Alexei Mukhin, the head of the Center for Political Information, admits that the Turkish President will change his point of view in relations with Russia, as soon as it will be profitable for him.
Eight months ago, Erdoğan took an extremely tough stance against Russia and sought favor in the West by all means.
For an extremely short period of time, the situation has switched the opposite.
There are no assurances that after a while the Turkish president does not again change its priorities.
I don’t think Erdoğan has entered a period of shuttle diplomacy.
Having found himself between the two poles - the West and Russia, he will try to get the maximum benefit for himself," Mukhin said.
In his opinion, the main task for the President of Turkey today is to maintain the political power in the country, and for this he just needs Russia and partnership relations with Vladimir Putin, so that the United States ceases to support Gülen.
Turkey needs both the EU and the U.S., which with its help controls the situation in Syria and in the Middle East.
Consensus with Turkey will therefore be reached,” the expert said.
Another point of view adheres to the head of the Central center of Asia and the Middle East Anna Glazova.
Now Turkey's relations with both the EU and the U.S. are quite tense, and this is primarily due primarily to the domestic policy pursued by Erdogan personally.
I think that this crisis in relations will be exacerbated, and against the background of this crisis, the Turkish leadership will try to carry out a reboot of politics both in the Middle East and in relations with Russia.
It is no coincidence that the President of Turkey, as well as a number of Turkish politicians say that they are ready to change their position on a number of issues on a number of issues on Syria, relations have been settled with Israel, there is an active political interaction with Iran,” Glazova explained, admitting that Erdogan’s next step will be to normalize relations with Yehypt.
According to her, the policy that Erdoğan currently pursues is a return to the course of a foreign policy concept, which Turkey followed before the beginning of the Arab revolutions.
“It is then that Turkey began to very actively develop its relations with the Middle East and the Asian region very actively.
Now there were very successful visits of the President of the President of Turkey to Russia and the countries of Africa have taken place.
Therefore, this vector of foreign policy will develop and contribute to strengthening Turkey’s positions both in the regional and international arena,” the expert believes.
Glazova believes that it will be incredibly difficult for Turkey to return to previous relations with the European Union, now the question is rather whether the EU will begin to strictly respond to Turkey, which can go to the opening of borders for the migrants and thereby create serious problems for Europe.
