The French court deposes the proposal to demolish stores in the Jungle refugee camp
The French court has rejected Calé’s proposal to demolish dozens of impregnable machining shops and restaurants in Jüngli’s camp where thousands of refugees live.
At this week's court hearings, Kale authorities said that the state should be able to demolish a total of 72 self-build shops, where people inside the camp sell goods, from cigarette to energy drinks, offer a haircut or sell tea for 50 ceremonies (40p) or mere merchandising meals for a few euros in self-immediate restaurants.
Kale Prefecture said that these places should be demolished, because they represent a parallel economy that does not pay taxes, and because they carry fire and sanitary risks.
Gumanitarian groups, told the court that shops and restaurants are life-sufficient, declaring free meals offered by a pro-governmental association and other groups don't give enough meals for a growing number in the harasse.
They said that homemade shops and restaurants often provide shelter and free meals for those in need.
Lille's court ruled that there was no legal basis to exorcise people driving 72 home make-up stores, but stated that Kale's prefecture "were completely understandable."
Judge Jean-François Molla said that groceries, cafloors and restaurants played a much larger role than simply feeding people who "live in extremely shaky conditions."
He said that the spaces were provided with tranquilibrium points for refugees and volunteers.
One of the sites, which was at risk of demolition, was a children's café of Jungle at the camp, which does not work for profit and offers 200 memorabilized lunches annoying a day, an English-Fóree French class and asylum consultations for hundreds of vulnerable unaccompanied unaccompanied minors.
more than 170 000 people signed a petition to protect a children's cauldron.
A letter sent to the Guardian stated that clhibiting "would be a disaster for these children, some of which are not older than the age of eight."
Two philanthropic institutions, helping Refugees and L'Auberge des Migrants on Friday, estimated that the number of Jüngley camp has risen and exceeded 9 000 people — the tallest in history - after a sharp increase in newly arrived during the summer.
