In Poland it is admitted that they will not buy fuel from “Gazprom”.
The state-owned Polish oil and gas company PGNIG does not preclude that in a few years gas supplies from the Russian Federation can become unattractive for it due to reorientation to fuel from Norway and other countries.
This was announced by the vice-president of the Polish company for finance Bohuslav Mazhets.
His words are conveyed by TASS.
“May it so happen that we will not completely buy gas (from “Gazprom”, we will now diversify our deliveries.
In the next four years, the main areas of deliveries for us will be the Vostok, raw materials from LNG-terminals and Norway,” Mazhets said.
At the present time, Gazprom occupies a dominant position in the supply of gas to Poland.
On Friday, August 12, PGNIG reported that the Russian supplier in the first half of 2016 supplied it with almost 5.3 billion cubic meters of gas, which is 30% higher than the level of in 2015.
The share of the Russian gas giant in PGNIG procurement increased to 49.8% compared to 40% a year earlier.
Recall that on July 22 the antimonopoly service of Poland did not agree on the creation of a joint venture of “Gazprom” and its European partners for the construction of the gas pipeline “Nord stream-2”.
At the same time this a month earlier, Poland left a request to “Gazprom” for the supply of 11 billion cubic meters of gas through “Nord stream-2”.
This was stated by the Deputy Chairman of the management of the holding Alexander Medvedev.
An auction for the capacity of "North Stream 2" will take place in 2017, and in the autumn of 2016 it is planned to conduct an auction for its laying.
At the end of May it was reported that "Gazprom" does not yet plan to take any actions in connection with the intentions of the Polish side to abandon long-term contracts for the supply of gas from Russia after 2022, said the deputy chairman of the company's board Alexander Medvedev.
He recalled that in the first quarter of this year, the Poles only increased gas purchases from the Russian Federation by 42%.
The Deputy Chairman of “Gazprom” added that the very fact of filing a Polish PGNIG application to the Stockholm arbitration court does not mean the termination of negotiations.
Earlier, the Polish company appealed to the judicial authority in order to achieve a decrease in the purchase price for Russian gas.
We will remind, the representative of the government of Poland on issues of gas and energy infrastructure Pyotr Naima said that the Polish authorities have decided not to renew the contract with “Gazprom” for the supply of gas.
The contract of Poland with “Gazprom” was signed in 1996 and expires in 2022.
In March, the representative of the government of Poland for gas and energy infrastructure, Peter Naimsky, said that his country in six years may refuse the supply of blue fuel from the east.
According to him, Warsaw plans to build a new terminal for the supply of liquefied natural gas Baltic Pipe, which will connect the country with the Norwegian shelf.
“Northern stream-2” envisages the construction of two lines of the offshore gas pipeline with a total capacity of 55 billion cubic meters of gas per year from Russia to Germany along the bottom of the Baltic Sea.
