How director David Lowrey managed to shoot a fantastically nice remake of the forgotten Disney film “Dragon Pete” in 1977, mediocre from the nose to the tip of the tail and memorized only with a mixture of animation and a feature-film, and to make him the most soulful film of this summer?
Nothing else a miracle helped.
In his piss, Lowry from the original, in fact, took only two things: an orphan Piet and a dragon.
Behind remained musical numbers and most of the strange jokes.
In this friendly fairytale read the traits borrowed from “Bumby”, “The Alien woman” and a lot of other cute children’s paintings.
The studio of Disney continues to successfully encourage the gallery of high-quality remakes of the 21st century.
Of course, “Pete and the dragon” isn’t as staggeringly beautiful as “Jungle Book”, and there is no skillful villain, which turned out to be Kate Blanchett as a stepmother in the new “Cinderella”.
But this naïvigable film no less successfully helps to believe in miracles and go-and-wedding in the best Disney traditions.
