The military leadership distorted the intelligence about ISIS, according to the congressional Commission
Officials from U.S. Central Command have changed intelligence reports to present a more optimistic picture of the war against the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria than the cause of the event on-situation on-site, a congressional commission said in a report released on Thursday.
An interim report of the operational group, established by the Republican chairpersons of the Federal Armed Affairs Committee, the Intelligence Committee and the Suburpose on Military Affiliation, found “a widespread discontinuity” among analysts of the Central Command’s intelligence, who argue that the leaders reproached their assessments of American actions aimed at defeating the Islamic State.
The central command, known as the Centcom, is the main military office in Tampa, the state of Florida, which oversees U.S. military operations in the Middle East and in Central Asia.
“Intelligence documents, approved by Centkom’s higher command, generally gave a more anti-optical image of US anti-terrorist actions than being confirmed by the real data from the scene and were stably more optimistic than an analysis provided by other special services.
