The ten-page report examines in detail the deficiencies continually arising in 2014 and 2015 in describing and analysing American actions aimed at educating the armed forces of Iraq provided by the Central Command.
Although it does not contain definitive evidence that members of the Obama administration were ordered to falsify the reports, he argues that analysts felt that the Centcom leadership compulsoriates them to pose a more optimistic view of a threat from an Islamic state, also known as ISIS or ISIL.
“Through in the first half of 2015, many press-releases, statements and testimonies of the Central Command in Congress were much more optimistic than the actual events.”
For example, an official of Centcom publicly stated that a major military attack aimed at Mosul could begin as early as April or May 2015.
Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, remains controlled by the Islamic State.
“Follow the months of the inquiral investigation is known for sure,” House member Mike Pompeo, a Kansas Republican said in a statement.
