Leitator-commander Patrick Evans, a Pentagon spokeswoman, said in a statement that the Department of Defense will not comment on the congressional report while the chief inspector’s investigation continues.
“Experts sometimes disagree on the interpretation of complex data, and the special services and the Ministry of Defense welcome a healthy dialogue on these vital topics related to national security.”
Consequence of the investigation goes beyond the question of whether the U.S.-made bombardment in Iraq and Syria achieves a target (as more and more representatives of the Ministry of Defense are now claiming).
But last year’s statements cast it to be questioned whether President Obama can rely on the fact that the Central Command provides truthful assessments of military operations in Iraq, Afghanistan, Libya and other crisis points.
Criticism of intelligence on the Islamic State escheates the controversy that took place more than a few years ago, when Scientcom intelligence analyst Gregory Hooker wrote a research article claiming that President George-Wucher W. Bush's administration because of the protest of many analysts spoke out in support of a small number of troops in Iraq and did not think what was going to be followed by an influencing in 2003.
Hooker also played a key role in the protest caused by intelligence on the Islamic State.
Hooker’s team came to the conclusion last year that despite public statements to the contrary, airstrikes on oil refineries belonging to the Islamic state did not lead to a significant deterioration in the financial situation of the terrorist group, because it has built temporary refineries to sell oil on the black market.
But the inauguration hasn’t been made available outside of the Central Command, according to the Times newspaper in September.
