Rebelled coffee shop returns to Greenpoint
The Werb coffin was fragrant, receiving only cash in Williamsgberg, which didn't have Wi-Fi, but served good coffee for 15 years.
Of course, she was pushed out of her room with a showcase on Bedford Avenue.
Store "Sope Sheri", which took the place of "Verb" in the summer of 2014, is engaged in the sale of soap in the form of cupcakes.
Local residents mourned her as the last loss in a renewing city.
Artist Molly Crabple tweeted: "New York is constantly changing, I know, but I still can't believe CBGB is dead, Chelsey's hotel has been demolished, and Verb is now a soap shop."
But at least New York is constantly constantly changing, it, in turn, is being renovated.
erranged Werb coffee shop opened to GreenPoint last November, less than a mile north of its original location.
In the new "Verbe" there is a Wi-Fi and take credit cards, but there are some of the same employees and a similar simple atmosphere.
In addition, the coffee is still good.
At the head of the new Verba is Sisko Rodriguez, an old employee of an old coffee shop who is the owner and general manager in a new place.
He began to work at Verb in 2001 and was there until the very end.
When one of the clients offered to sponsor it in a new place, he said that he would be happy to do it.
Rodriguez, which is 36 years of age, left a lot in the same form.
"Verb" works with the same bagled distributor and bought the same model of a coffee machine.
In it, chandeliers are installed from the old Verba.
Rodriguez said that he would also like to experiment.
He offered new meals.
The menu presents sandwiches with bacon, egg and cheese, and there are toppings from capers or fried onions.
There are spree desecrecies from brown Rice.
In the coffee shop they no longer serve a beer, but now they prepare freshly squeezed juices.
After nine months, Rodriguez said that he drew attention to an invariable flow of old regular customers.
“Many former clients were also squeezed out with prices from Williamsburg,” he said.
At one of the recent resurrections Rebecca Oliveira was one of those clients.
Oliveira, who is 34 years old, previously lived in Williamsburg and often visited the Werb.
Even after she moved to GreenPoint, she went there anyway.
After cloubling, she said that she stopped drinking coffee for some time.
“I realized that it was the coffee and the atmosphere that impressed me at Verba,” she said.
Now she again drinks coffee, although on that day instead of the usual ice-coffee she ordered a blended tea, another novelty.
Tom Rosenthal, another long-running client, has faithfully visited Verb since 2003.
He walked in front of the work and ordered the same thing invariably.
“At the epode fifteen minutes, every day, as in the hour, I came, and for me prepared my beagle with butter and with all the fillings,” he said.
Rodriguez said that he wanted to experiment and injected new meals on the menu.
Today Rosenthal still makes a regular order, but someday he will change something using bacon, eggs or cheese on a beaglay with all the fillings.
Rodriguez said that he did not offend the fact that the soap-wash once took possession of an old place.
What’s more, Sope Sheri owners have proposed to make special soap for Verba, using coffee coffee made in a ferrous method, as an ingredient.
“I don’t think that we may agree to their proposal,” Rodriguez said.
Permanent residents of Grinpolte of Polish origin also chose this coffee shop.
One of these new clients translated the menu into Polish, and Rodriguez said that he planned to find a place for him in a showcase, so that his new neighbors felt desirable.
A new place has other benefits.
The local point for the sale of the juice plans to spill on the bottles and sell coffee, prepared in a cold way.
There is a possibility that the coffee shop will connect the neighboring building.
For many customers it sufficimarily that the new "Verb" will include in the menu their favorite meals.
Joseph Witt was a regular customer and shared that he was grooped when they closed.
When the coffee shop opened in a new place, Witt said that by going inside and ordering a cup of coffee, he felt as if he had returned home.
“How long I’ve wanted to feel this fragrance,” he said.
Depressed meal of depression in "Damn meal"
In March of 1933, shortly after the ascensing as president, Franklin Delano Roosevelt sat down dining at the Oval Office.
Being a gourmet, President Rusewelt loved the Fifth Avenue dishes, such as foie greera and meriland turbulent soup.
Its menu on this day was happier: stuffed eggs in tomato sauce, mashed mashed potatoes, mashed potatoes and pusted pudding pound.
"This was an act of culinary solidarity with people who have suffered," said Jane Zieguelman.
Her Husband Andrew Coe added: “This was also a message to the Americans about how to ate.”
Spouses living in Brooklyn Heights are food history experts.
Coke’s latest book, “The Stork in China”, was devoted to Chinese cuisine in America, and Zieguelman told the story of life in a removable apartment in the Lower East Side through the meal in his book “House 97 on Fruit Garden Street”.
Their new co-joint meadow work, which will be published Tuesday by Harper, is the history of American meals in the Great Depression.
Exhiating to some extent its own culinary soristic solidarity, they met a reporter for dinner at Eisenberg's Sandwich Shop, a measly simple diner in the Flatyron Quarter, which has been operating since 1929, the Year of the Exchange Collapse.
Ziegelman, 54, ordered butterfly cheese and a sandwreck with pieces of olives, and Coe, 57, took turkey, mashed potatoes and a vegetable mixture.
