Ziegelman recalls: "She seriously poisoned the people."
There was an unpleasant case with paritrols.
When the stove in Eisenberg's was deserted, they and Coke took the dessert, which they cooked at the house: dessert from prunes that President Rwewelt ate for lunch.
“It’s referred to as prunes muss,” Siegelman said, adding that it was a common technique to give modest recipes catchy names.
There weren't swollen crees, only prunes, a little flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
Everyone reluctually immersed a tablespoon into a viscous, brown, sticky substance.
Surprisingly, it didn’t be bad.
Cow asked for add-ons twice.
With the voice of the confident compiler of the budget, he said to his wife: “Small chopped walnuts would not hurt here.”
