NASA: Vene d'éré was once inhabited
Venus may have ever been habitable, according to the estimates of NASA’s new climatic model.
Scientists from the Nava Institute for the Expectation of Goddard developed a model to study last Venus, using tools and algorithms similar to those used by climatologists.
The model was designed to determine whether on Venus, its hot annoyed surface and an atmosphere saturated with cenocentered with carbon dioxide, could ever exist conditions like terresoppier.
Scientists previously assumed that a small velocity of Venus is explained by its dense atmosphere.
But the last simulation suggests that Venus could probably have had a divided atmosphere like an terrescub and still rotate slowly.
The model also looked at the topography of early Venus.
There have probably been more anointing regions on the planet than on Earth, but once there was enough water and enough geographical variability to create conditions for life and prevent rapid evaporation.
Early Venus also benefited the sun, which was 30 per cent dimmer.
“According to the GISS model, VENA’s slow-wheel rotation exposes its daily side to the sun for almost two months at one time,” GISS scientist Anthony Del Genio said.
This commemorates the surface of the planet and produces the rain, which creates a thick layer of clouds, acting as an umbrella that protects the surface of the planet from an essential part of the sun's heat.
The result is average climinary temperatures, which, surprisingly, a few degrees is colder than these days on Earth.
The model shows the history of Venus, similar with the development, which was previously assumed by specialists in the study of the planets.
Despite the fact that Venya once may have been habitable, for several billion years it was exposed to too much heat and ultraviolet radiation.
Its ocean evaporated and its water vapor was burned with ultraviolet radiation.
When its hydrogen evaporated, there is only a dense atmosphere of carbon dioxide with a deadly greenhouse effect - that is, Venus, as we know it.
The researchers shared the results of their new climatic model of Venus in the journal Geophysical Research Letters.
