Kepler Measllates Rotation Rating Ratives in Pleiades
Kepler’s latest mission of the Space Telescope helped astronomers assemble the most complete catalog of speeds of rotation of stars that are part of Pleiades.
The new data helps astronomers understand why stars in the cluster rotate at different velocities, as well as periods of rotation correcrement with the formation of the planets.
“We hope that comparing our results with other stellar clusters, we will learn more about the relationship between the mass of the star, its age and even the history of its solar system,” Luisa Ridal, a researcher at the Center for Infrared and Analysis, said in a press release.
Pleiades are located 445 light-annual years from the Ground.
Many stars in the congregation now only reach maturity; they were born about 125 million years ago.
They revolve as fast as they will never again.
At their peak rotation speeds, these growing stars emit large amounts of stellar wind.
Traveling through the magnetic fields of the stars, these winds have a bragging effect on stars.
As the star's genuariates, its rotational velocity begins to squeeze.
Kepler measures the speed of rotation of each star, tracing the time between recognizable stalk squirrels, such as sun squats.
The newest velocity catalog of rotational velocities leads us to specifies that the more massive stars rotate more slowly.
Riball compares the stars to the dancers: larger ones make pirouettes more slower than the smaller ones.
“In ballet Pleiades we observe that those that rotate slowly are usually more massive, while those that rotate faster usually belong to very light stars,” Riballe said.
Since Pleiades cluster is so close, it serves as an ideal space laboratory for comparing the properties of the stars with rotational velocities.
“The Pleiada Steering Cluster provides a starting point for theoretical models of rotation of stars, both junior and the oldest,” Riballe said.
We still want to learn a lot about how, where and why the stars squeeze the velocity of their rotation and hung their “dance shoes” figuratively.
Riballl and her colleagues outlined in detail their latest Pleiades Rotate velocities in three new articles, which will soon be published in an Astronomical Journal.
