The main coach of the Russian national football team was Stanislav Cherchesov
At the meeting of the Executive Committee of the Russian football Union it was made a decision on the appointment to the position of head coach of the Russian national football team Stanislav Cherchenov.
The contract with him will be calculated for two years - until the completion of the 2018 world Cup.
On August 11, the RFU Executive Committee appointed Stanislav Cherchesov as the head coach of the Russian national football team.
This is a well-known football player, who previously competed in Spartak Moscow as a goalkeeper, as well as a coach with a solid experience, who was a mentor of such Russian teams as the same “Spartacus”, Pearl-Sochi, Terek, Amkar, Dynamo, as well as the Warsaw “Legia”, which became the winner of the national championship and the national championship.
It is reported that the contract with Cherchesov will be signed in the very near future and is designed for two years.
Thus, it is he who has the obligation to prepare the national team for the world championship of 2018.
It is known that Miroslav Romashchenko, Gintaras Stauce, Vladimir Panikov will enter his coaching headquarters.
There is information, according to which the salary of the entire coaching staff will be 2.6 billion euros a year.
According to media reports, the leadership of the RFU has set before Stanislav Cherchesov a goal to bring the Russian national team to the semi-finals and win medals.
As stated by the trainer himself, as a priority task, he considers the creation of a competitive environment in the team and is going to give the players the opportunity to express themselves.
Also, according to Cherchesov, he is glad that the choice fell on him.
The first match under the leadership of Cherchesov will the Russian national team will be held on August 31 in Antalya - it will be a friendly meeting with the Turkish team.
We will remind, earlier the post of the head coach of the Russian national football team was occupied by Leonid Slutsky, who combined him with coaching in the CSKA club.
He announced his resignation from his post after the failed performance of the national team of Russia at Euro 2016 in France.
At the same time, he took the responsibility for the bad result of the national team on himself.
Squeezed between Angola and South Africa, Nambia was badly damaged during a long struggle against apartheid.
Nevertheless, after gaining independence from South Africa in 1990, a 2.4-million-dollar country has achieved great strides, especially in the last few years.
The main reason for Namib's success is that the government pays a lot of attention to education.
While people in the developed countries are self-accustomed to free primary and secondary education, in many poor countries in secondary and even primary schools require tuition fees.
Moreover, Governments are often advised to introduce tutorial fees to “reimburse costs”.
In Namib, however, primary education is free of charge in Nambia.
Moreover, since 2015, secondary education has also become free of charge.
The authorities of the country are also actively working in other important areas.
Efforts to destroy malaria have shortened annual cases by 97% over the past ten years.
Contrary to the international trend of rising inequalities, in Nambia it is shrinking.
Compared compared to the indicator, Gini coefficient, which is a standard inequity of income inequality, fell by 15 points.
Then, 23 years ago, this figure was one of the tallest in the world.
And the level of poverty decreased from 69% in 1993 to less than 30%.
The number of people living in conditions of extreme poverty (less than $ 1.90 per a day) has also decreased, from almost 53% to less than 23%.
Namibia also has the highest free-to-me-voil rating in Africa, according to “Reporters Without Borders”.
The country was much ahead of the United United States and other rich states.
In addition, the country has a favorable rating - one of the tallest in Africa - in the corrupt Persecure Index, which consists of human rights activists from Transparency International.
Even now, when prices for raw materials are falling, Namibia manages to maintain a high rate of growth.
At the Same time, the debt-to-GDP ratio is about 25%, which is less than 1/3 of debt in the U.S. and most European countries.
Of course, Nambia also has some problems.
Unemployment, at least according to traditional valuation methods, remains quite high - almost 28%.
And, like other countries in the region, in Namibia the incidence of HIV/JCAD - almost 17% of the population are infected.
According to the World Bank, Namibium is among the above-of-middle-than-middle-income countries, but the authorities insist that their state is actually a developing country.
Namibia, of course, faces some difficulties inherent in underdeveloped economies.
The state has to develop a very large and arrogant country with a small population.
On the territory of the country in the deserts are inhabited by isolated nomadic tribe of hunter-gatherers, which in one way or another should be integrated into modern society, without loss of their identity.
Integration of people, who a quarter of a century ago were on the periphery of the global economy is not an easy task for any country.
For Namybia, this is even more more difficult: just like any other country in the world, it is faced with the consequences of climate change.
However, Nambia did not dare to put their hands off, the country solves the problems facing it.
Almost immediately after gaining independence, the authorities engaged in creating a diversified economy and a cohesive society.
It is noteworthy that competing political factions have come together in order to work for a common good.
Indeed, Nymbia provides cheap medical care not only to its own citizens, but also to its neighbors.
Approximately 15-20% of visitors to medical institutions in the northern regions of Namybia are Angol citizens.
Nobody in Namibiya talks about the construction of a wall that would separate the country from poor and corrupt neighbourhood.
The Namibians realized that the neighbors are not chosen, and therefore decided not to conflict with them, but to cooperate - to share scarce water resources, to take part in regional medical projects, to encourage investment and the exchange of students.
In addition, the Government of Namibia rightly judgments that rich natural resources can easily become a cursed, promoting the enrichment of the few at the expense of many.
The authorities understand that the proceeds from the natural wealth of Namibas need to be invested in infrastructure and productive capacities.
They are also aware that it would be irresponsible not to strive to obtain the maximum profit from the sale of the country's resources, and are revising investment and mining contracts to make sure it is.
Transparientity is crucial to this process, so the government’s strong support for freedom of speech is so important.
As noted by the head of state Hage Heinhob, the media often say what he does not like.
But after the struggle for freedom from apartheid in South Africa, Nambia must defend the freedoms received, the president said.
Not all of Namibia’s key resources are exhausted.
Some, such such as fishing, are revolved, and the government is making every effort to preserve and harsect them.
Every year, more than a million foreign tourists visit the country.
Namybia shows that even those countries that begin with serious facilities - racism, the colonial system, the inequalities and a low level of development - can take the path to common prosperity.
Its achievement deserves international acknowledgment and imitation.
Many have heard of "Gilding of Varna" - the most ancient golden objects created by a person (4600 - 4200 BC).
Perhaps, this was the loudest archaeological sensation of the 1970s: in Bulgaria, during the excavations of the Varna necropolis, a fantastic number of golden artifacts was found - more than three thousand with a total weight of 6 kilograms.
From one burial, known as "grave No. 43", was extracted 990 gold objects weighing more than 1.5 kilograms.
However, the official status of the world's oldest gold may soon move to an inconspicuous beckon discovered by archaeologists in July this year.
And again in Bulgaria - in the pre-history settlement, known as Tell Yunácite.
In contrast to the Varna, the port city, Tell Yunacite is located away from the sea, near the village of Yunazite in the Pazardjik district.
Bustina is a golden strip, folded into a ring.
Yavor Boyadzhiyev, head of excavations at Tella Yunazite, dated the discovery of 4600 BC and stated that it is older than Varna’s gold for at least 200 years.
Treasures of Varna, let us recall, are dated in about the same period, 4600 - 4200 BC.
Technologists, allowing to date the metal with impeccable accuracy, currently does not exist.
How do you know what kind of gold is more ancient?
Obscirably Professor Boyadzhiyev knows more than told Reuters: “I am quite confident that Yunáccete gold is older than Varna’s gold.”
It’s a discovery of an extremely important thing: it’s a tiny item, but it’s big enough to take a proper place in history.
In an interview with Reuters, Boyadzhiev said that in the near future, scientists will analyze the composition of the metal, as well as to re-expare and more thoroughly the archaeological stratfume, where a gold artibrium was found.
This will help date the beetrox with maximum accuracy as possible.
The site of the find also explains a lot.
Tell Yunazite is a well-fatured, even if you’ve never heard of it.
At the present time Tell Yunazcate is considered the oldest "city" of Europe.
Tella was founded by descendants of people who migrated to Europe from Anatolia (Modern-Januquet Turkey) in 7 millennium BC.
During the following centuries in this region turbulently developed the processing of metals, from the development of the first metallurgical sensual skills to the creation of a full-fledged industry.
In domestic historiography for this archaeological community there is a special name: Balkan-Carpathian metallurgical province.
In 5 millennium BC, urban-type settlements began to appear around the centers of metalworking.
Now they are called the most ancient cities in Europe.
Of these, Tell Yunazite is perhaps the most antiquity, since artibrels of 4900 BC have been found here.
I'd call Tell Yunacita the prototype of a modern city, although it is a very ancient settlement, even compared with Mesopotamia.
“This is the place older than Sumer by a thousand years,” says Boyadzhiyev, referring to the generally accepted opinion that urban culture originated in the south of Mesopotamia - modern Iraq.
There arose the first cities in the usual sense of the word.
Expeditheated Tell Junocite has been ongoing since 1976.
During this time, archaeologists excavated almost 12 hectare, this is about a third of the proposed total area of the settlement.
Remnants of the serpent wall were found, which once reached three meter in height.
From the houses and the workshops, there is not much preserved, but archaeologists clearly distinguish the streets, public buildings, urban dense buildings, a “business center” and residential outskirts.
Tell Yunáctace is often referred to as "Birdy City": dozens of clay filaments of birds have been found here.
All the birds are depicted in a state of tranquility, hence the assumption that they were not decorative objects, but an object of a local cult.
"Bird city" was demolished around 4100 BC - perhaps Indo-European tribes that came from the north-east.
Nomads didn’t have cities, but there were horses and weapons.
Three-meter serf walls did not stop them.
Archaeologists have found obvious signs of a severe massacre: the remains of the elderly, women and children with traces inflicted by battle axes were found inside the houses.
New finds and new facts appear annually, changing the usual ideas about the ancient history of humankind.
Bulgarian archaeologists are increasingly not talking about individual crops, but about the "lost" first civilization of Europe, which originated in the Balkans.
A powerful stimulus in the search for this civilization was the “Green of Varna”, found relatively recently, in 1972.
It’s logical that Varna’s gold luxitella should have preceded—something like a tiny golden beech, created in Tella Yunácite.
